   {
    "id": "lem-cg-bruhat-right-exchange-and-augmentation",
    "kind": "lemma",
    "title": "Right-handed strong exchange and the augmentation step for reduced subwords",
    "deps": [
     "def-cg-bruhat-order-by-reflection-chains",
     "thm-cg-root-inversion-formulas-and-strong-exchange",
     "thm-hh-parabolic-minimal-representatives-and-length-additivity",
     "def-hh-coxeter-matrix-word-group-and-length",
     "def-cg-canonical-reflection-homomorphism",
     "def-group"
    ],
    "statement": "Let $W$ be the presented Coxeter group with length $\\ell$ ([[def-hh-coxeter-matrix-word-group-and-length]]), reflection set $T$ ([[def-cg-canonical-reflection-homomorphism]]) and Bruhat order $\\le$ ([[def-cg-bruhat-order-by-reflection-chains]]).\n\n**(1) Right-handed strong exchange.** Let $u=s_1\\cdots s_q$ be a reduced expression and let $t\\in T$ satisfy $\\ell(ut)<\\ell(u)$. Then there is exactly one index $i\\in\\{1,\\dots,q\\}$ with\n$$ut=s_1\\cdots\\widehat{s_i}\\cdots s_q,\\qquad t=(s_q\\cdots s_{i+1})\\,s_i\\,(s_{i+1}\\cdots s_q),$$and $ut\\to u$ is a Bruhat edge.\n\n**(2) Augmentation lemma.** Let $w=s_1\\cdots s_q$ be a reduced expression and let $u\\in W$, $u\\ne w$, be such that some reduced expression of $u$ is a subword of $s_1\\cdots s_q$. Then there exists $v\\in W$ with\n$$u\\to v\\quad(\\text{so }u<v\\text{ and }\\ell(v)=\\ell(u)+1),$$such that some reduced expression of $v$ is a subword of $s_1\\cdots s_q$. More precisely, choose a reduced subword expression $u=s_{i_1}\\cdots s_{i_k}$, $1\\le i_1<\\cdots<i_k\\le q$, for which $i_k$ is as small as possible and put $t:=(s_q\\cdots s_{i_k+1})s_{i_k}(s_{i_k+1}\\cdots s_q)\\in T$; then $ut$ is the product of the subword of $s_1\\cdots s_q$ obtained by deleting only the positions $i_1,\\dots,i_{k-1}$, and this word is a reduced expression of $ut$ of length $\\ell(u)+1$.",
    "strategy": "**(1)** Apply the left-handed strong exchange of [[thm-cg-root-inversion-formulas-and-strong-exchange]] (3) to the element $u^{-1}=s_q\\cdots s_1$, a reduced expression by [[thm-hh-parabolic-minimal-representatives-and-length-additivity]] (3) ($\\ell(x)=\\ell(x^{-1})$), and to the reflection $t$: the hypothesis reads $\\ell(tu^{-1})=\\ell((ut)^{-1})=\\ell(ut)<\\ell(u)=\\ell(u^{-1})$. The theorem gives a unique index $i'$ with $tu^{-1}$ equal to $s_q\\cdots s_1$ with its $i'$-th letter deleted and $t$ equal to the corresponding prefix reflection of that word. Inverting both identities ([[def-group]]: $(xy)^{-1}=y^{-1}x^{-1}$, $s_j^{-1}=s_j$) and setting $i=q+1-i'$ gives the displayed identities and the uniqueness of $i$. Finally $ut\\to u$ because $u=(ut)t$ with $t\\in T$ and $\\ell(u)>\\ell(ut)$.\n\n**(2)** Write the deleted positions as $i_1<\\cdots<i_k$ and let $i:=i_k$, with $t$ as displayed; $t$ is a conjugate of $s_i$, hence lies in $T$. Multiplying the word for $u$ on the right by $t$ and cancelling the tail, $ut=(u)(s_q\\cdots s_{i+1})s_i(s_{i+1}\\cdots s_q)$ is the word $s_1\\cdots s_q$ with only the positions $i_1,\\dots,i_{k-1}$ deleted; this is an explicit word of length $q-k+1=\\ell(u)+1$, so $\\ell(ut)\\le\\ell(u)+1$, and $\\ell(ut)\\ne\\ell(u)$ by the parity clause of [[def-cg-bruhat-order-by-reflection-chains]] (4).\n\nWe claim $\\ell(ut)>\\ell(u)$; suppose instead that $\\ell(ut)<\\ell(u)$. Applying part (1) to the reduced expression of $u$ formed by its retained letters and to $t$ gives a uniquely determined retained position $p$ with $ut$ equal to that word with the position $p$ additionally deleted, and with $t$ the conjugate of $s_p$ by the suffix of the word after position $p$. There are two cases; this is the case analysis of Bjorner-Brenti, Lemma 2.2.1 (printed pp. 33-34), which was re-read on the raw PDF pages 42-43 so that the deleted-letter (hat) convention could be verified.\n\n*Case A: $p>i$.* Then the suffix of the word for $u$ after position $p$ is $s_{p+1}\\cdots s_q$, so $t=(s_q\\cdots s_{p+1})s_p(s_{p+1}\\cdots s_q)$; since $t^2=1$ in $W$,\n$$w=wt^2=(s_1\\cdots s_q)(s_q\\cdots s_{i+1})s_i(s_{i+1}\\cdots s_q)(s_q\\cdots s_{p+1})s_p(s_{p+1}\\cdots s_q)=s_1\\cdots\\widehat{s_i}\\cdots\\widehat{s_p}\\cdots s_q,$$a word for $w$ of length $q-2$, contradicting $\\ell(w)=q$.\n\n*Case B: $p<i$.* Write the word for $u$ as $Z\\,s_p\\,Y\\,(s_{i+1}\\cdots s_q)$, where $s_p$ is the retained letter at position $p$, $Y$ collects the retained letters strictly between positions $p$ and $i$, and $Z$ those before $p$. Then $t=(s_q\\cdots s_{i+1})\\,Y^{-1}s_pY\\,(s_{i+1}\\cdots s_q)$, so using $u=ut^2=ut\\cdot t$ and re-multiplying, $u$ equals the word $s_1\\cdots s_q$ with the positions $\\{i_1,\\dots,i_{k-1},p\\}$ deleted; this is a word of length $q-k=\\ell(u)$ representing $u$, hence a reduced subword expression whose largest deleted position is $\\max(i_{k-1},p)<i$, contradicting the minimality of $i_k=i$.\n\nBoth cases being impossible, $\\ell(ut)=\\ell(u)+1$, so $u\\to ut$ and $v:=ut$ has all the stated properties, the displayed word being reduced of length $\\ell(v)$. No use of the Axiom of Choice is made: the extremal choice minimises a nonempty finite set of natural numbers, and the index in (1) is unique.",
    "provenance": {
     "statement": "literature-derived",
     "proof": "ai-altered"
    },
    "sources": {
     "references": [
      {
       "title": "Anders Bjorner and Francesco Brenti, Combinatorics of Coxeter Groups (Graduate Texts in Mathematics 231, Springer 2005; author-hosted complete PDF)",
       "url": "https://sites.math.washington.edu/~billey/classes/reflection.groups/references/EntireBook.pdf",
       "locator": "Section 2.2, printed pp. 33-34: Lemma 2.2.1 with its complete proof (choice of the subword with minimal last deleted position, the reflection $t=s_q\\cdots s_{i_k}\\cdots s_q$, and the two contradiction cases (2.8) and (2.9))"
      },
      {
       "title": "Tom Denton, Lifting property and poset structure of finite Coxeter groups (UC Davis MAT 280 lecture notes, 26 January 2009)",
       "url": "https://www.math.ucdavis.edu/~anne/WQ2009/MAT280-Lecture9.pdf",
       "locator": "Lemma 1 (printed p. 1): the augmentation lemma for a reduced subword of a reduced word, with conclusion $v>u$, $\\ell(v)=\\ell(u)+1$; Proposition 5 (p. 2) for the lifting input used implicitly in the exchange step"
      },
      {
       "title": "Carl Marberg, MATH 6150F Coxeter systems and Iwahori-Hecke algebras, Lecture 11: More about Bruhat order (HKUST, Spring 2017)",
       "url": "https://www.math.hkust.edu.hk/~emarberg/teaching/2017/Math6150F/lectures/11_Math6150F_Spring2017.pdf",
       "locator": "Lecture 11, printed p. 1: the strong exchange condition with uniqueness of the deleted index in the reduced case, the input to (1)"
      }
     ]
    },
    "dependency_level": 12
   },
   {
    "id": "thm-cg-bruhat-subword-characterization",
    "kind": "theorem",
    "title": "The subword characterization of Bruhat order and its independence of the reduced expression",
    "deps": [
     "lem-cg-bruhat-right-exchange-and-augmentation",
     "def-cg-bruhat-order-by-reflection-chains",
     "thm-hh-coxeter-exchange-deletion-and-faithfulness",
     "def-hh-coxeter-matrix-word-group-and-length",
     "def-group"
    ],
    "statement": "Let $w\\in W$ have reduced expression $w=s_1\\cdots s_q$ ([[def-hh-coxeter-matrix-word-group-and-length]]) and let $\\le$ be the Bruhat order ([[def-cg-bruhat-order-by-reflection-chains]]). Then, for every $u\\in W$,\n$$u\\le w\\iff\\text{there exist }1\\le i_1<\\cdots<i_k\\le q\\text{ with }u=s_{i_1}\\cdots s_{i_k},$$and the indices may be chosen with $k=\\ell(u)$, so that $s_{i_1}\\cdots s_{i_k}$ is a reduced expression of $u$. Consequently the following are equivalent for all $u,w\\in W$: (a) $u\\le w$; (b) every reduced expression of $w$ has a subword that is a reduced expression of $u$; (c) some reduced expression of $w$ has a subword that is a reduced expression of $u$. In particular the Bruhat order is completely described by any one fixed reduced expression of $w$, and $1\\le w$ for every $w$ (the empty subword).",
    "strategy": "**(1) Chains give subwords.** Let $u=x_0\\to x_1\\to\\cdots\\to x_m=w$ be a chain exhibiting $u\\le w$, with $x_{j+1}=x_jt_j$, $t_j\\in T$, $\\ell(x_{j+1})>\\ell(x_j)$; the case $m=0$ ($u=w$) is trivial. Prove by downward induction on $j$ that $x_j$ is the product of a subword of $s_1\\cdots s_q$. Suppose $x_{j+1}$ is the product of the subword at positions $P_{j+1}\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots,q\\}$. If the word $(s_p)_{p\\in P_{j+1}}$ is not reduced, repeatedly apply the two-letter deletion property ([[thm-hh-coxeter-exchange-deletion-and-faithfulness]] (3)) to replace it by a reduced expression of $x_{j+1}$ obtained by deleting letters of $P_{j+1}$; the result is again a subword of $s_1\\cdots s_q$. Applying the right-handed strong exchange of [[lem-cg-bruhat-right-exchange-and-augmentation]] (1) to this reduced expression of $x_{j+1}$ and the reflection $t_j$ (legal since $\\ell(x_{j+1}t_j)=\\ell(x_j)<\\ell(x_{j+1})$) exhibits $x_j=x_{j+1}t_j$ as that word with one letter deleted, hence as the product of a subword of $s_1\\cdots s_q$. At the end $x_0=u$ is the product of a subword; applying the deletion property once more to that subword word produces a reduced subword expression of $u$.\n\n**(2) Subwords give chains.** Assume $u=s_{i_1}\\cdots s_{i_k}$ with $1\\le i_1<\\cdots<i_k\\le q$; reducing that word by two-letter deletions if necessary, we may suppose it is a reduced expression of $u$ (deletions only remove positions, so the result is still a subword of $s_1\\cdots s_q$). Induct on $d:=\\ell(w)-\\ell(u)\\ge0$. If $d=0$ then $u=w$ and $u\\le w$ by reflexivity. If $d>0$ then $u\\ne w$, so the augmentation lemma ([[lem-cg-bruhat-right-exchange-and-augmentation]] (2)) applied to the fixed reduced expression $w=s_1\\cdots s_q$ produces $v\\in W$ with $u\\to v$, $\\ell(v)=\\ell(u)+1$ and $v$ the product of a reduced subword of the same word; the induction hypothesis applies to $v$ (length gap $d-1$) and gives $v\\le w$, whence $u\\le v\\le w$.\n\n**(3) Expression independence.** Statement (1) is formulated for an arbitrary reduced expression $w=s_1\\cdots s_q$, so (a), (b), (c) are equivalent. This is Bjorner-Brenti, Theorem 2.2.2 with Corollary 2.2.3, whose augmentation input (Lemma 2.2.1) is proved above rather than quoted. The empty subword gives $1\\le w$. No use of the Axiom of Choice is made: the induction runs on natural numbers and the deletions are performed on the current explicit word.",
    "provenance": {
     "statement": "literature-derived",
     "proof": "ai-altered"
    },
    "sources": {
     "references": [
      {
       "title": "Anders Bjorner and Francesco Brenti, Combinatorics of Coxeter Groups (Graduate Texts in Mathematics 231, Springer 2005; author-hosted complete PDF)",
       "url": "https://sites.math.washington.edu/~billey/classes/reflection.groups/references/EntireBook.pdf",
       "locator": "Section 2.2, printed pp. 34-35: Theorem 2.2.2 (the Subword Property, both directions) and Corollary 2.2.3 (independence of the chosen reduced expression)"
      },
      {
       "title": "Tom Denton, Lifting property and poset structure of finite Coxeter groups (UC Davis MAT 280 lecture notes, 26 January 2009)",
       "url": "https://www.math.ucdavis.edu/~anne/WQ2009/MAT280-Lecture9.pdf",
       "locator": "Theorem 2 (printed p. 1): the Subword Property, stated as $u\\le w$ iff there is a reduced expression of $u$ that is a subword of the given reduced word of $w$"
      },
      {
       "title": "Carl Marberg, MATH 6150F Coxeter systems and Iwahori-Hecke algebras, Lecture 11: More about Bruhat order (HKUST, Spring 2017)",
       "url": "https://www.math.hkust.edu.hk/~emarberg/teaching/2017/Math6150F/lectures/11_Math6150F_Spring2017.pdf",
       "locator": "Lecture 11, printed p. 1: the theorem that $v\\le w$ iff indices $1\\le i_1<\\cdots<i_q\\le r$ with $v=s_{i_1}\\cdots s_{i_q}$ exist for some, equivalently every, reduced expression $w=s_1\\cdots s_r$"
      },
      {
       "title": "Grant T. Barkley, Bruhat order and applications, Lecture 3 (CMND lecture notes, author-hosted)",
       "url": "https://gtbarkley.org/cmnd/Lecture3Notes.pdf",
       "locator": "Corollary 1.6 (printed p. 2): one fixed reduced word of $v$ suffices to test $u\\le v$, proved by following a path in the Bruhat graph and using strong exchange plus deletion"
      }
     ]
    },
    "dependency_level": 13
   },
   {
    "id": "lem-cg-bruhat-chain-refinement-and-gradedness",
    "kind": "lemma",
    "title": "Finiteness of Bruhat intervals, the chain refinement property, and grading by length",
    "deps": [
     "thm-cg-bruhat-subword-characterization",
     "lem-cg-bruhat-right-exchange-and-augmentation",
     "def-cg-bruhat-order-by-reflection-chains",
     "def-hh-coxeter-matrix-word-group-and-length",
     "def-natural-numbers"
    ],
    "statement": "Let $u\\le v$ in $W$ ([[def-cg-bruhat-order-by-reflection-chains]]) and put $[u,v]:=\\{x\\in W:u\\le x\\le v\\}$.\n\n**(1) Finiteness.** $[u,v]$ is finite; more precisely, for every reduced expression $v=s_1\\cdots s_q$ there is an injection $[u,v]\\to\\{0,1\\}^q$, so $|[u,v]|\\le 2^{\\ell(v)}$.\n\n**(2) Chain refinement.** If $u<v$ there exist $x_0,\\dots,x_k\\in W$ with\n$$u=x_0<x_1<\\cdots<x_k=v,\\qquad \\ell(x_i)=\\ell(u)+i\\quad(0\\le i\\le k);$$in particular $k=\\ell(v)-\\ell(u)$ and every step $x_{i-1}\\to x_i$ is a Bruhat edge whose length increases by exactly one.\n\n**(3) Grading.** Every maximal chain in $[u,v]$ has exactly $\\ell(v)-\\ell(u)$ strict steps, that is, $\\ell(v)-\\ell(u)+1$ elements; hence $[u,v]$ is a graded poset with rank function $x\\mapsto\\ell(x)-\\ell(u)$. In particular, if $x<y$ and no $z$ satisfies $x<z<y$, then $\\ell(y)=\\ell(x)+1$.",
    "strategy": "**(1)** Fix a reduced expression $v=s_1\\cdots s_q$. For $x\\in[u,v]$ the subword criterion ([[thm-cg-bruhat-subword-characterization]]) applied to $x\\le v$ provides at least one reduced subword expression of $x$ inside $s_1\\cdots s_q$; assign to $x$ the lexicographically first subset of $\\{1,\\dots,q\\}$ with this property (a determinate rule on a nonempty finite set of subsets, so no choice is involved). The map is injective because the subset determines $x$ as the product of its letters in the given order. This is Bjorner-Brenti, Corollary 2.2.4.\n\n**(2)** Induct on $d:=\\ell(v)-\\ell(u)$. If $d=0$ then $u=v$ and the one-element chain works. For $d>0$, fix a reduced expression $v=s_1\\cdots s_q$ and a reduced subword expression of $u$ in it; the augmentation lemma ([[lem-cg-bruhat-right-exchange-and-augmentation]] (2)) gives $x_1$ with $u\\to x_1$, $\\ell(x_1)=\\ell(u)+1$, and $x_1$ the product of a reduced subword of the same word $s_1\\cdots s_q$. By the subword criterion $x_1\\le v$, and $\\ell(v)-\\ell(x_1)=d-1$, so the induction hypothesis applies to $(x_1,v)$; prepending the edge $u\\to x_1$ gives the claim. This is Bjorner-Brenti, Theorem 2.2.6 ('follows directly from Lemma 2.2.1 and the Subword Property'), with the reduction carried out explicitly on the fixed word.\n\n**(3)** Along a strict step the length strictly increases (clause (2) of the definition item), so a chain from $u$ to $v$ with $m$ strict steps satisfies $\\ell(v)\\ge\\ell(u)+m$, i.e. $m\\le\\ell(v)-\\ell(u)$; part (2) attains equality, so a maximal chain has exactly $\\ell(v)-\\ell(u)$ steps. A strict pair $x<y$ with nothing strictly between is a maximal chain with one step in $[x,y]$, so $\\ell(y)-\\ell(x)=1$. This is the graded-poset remark following Bjorner-Brenti, Theorem 2.2.6 and Marberg's gradedness corollary. No use of the Axiom of Choice is made.",
    "provenance": {
     "statement": "literature-derived",
     "proof": "ai-altered"
    },
    "sources": {
     "references": [
      {
       "title": "Anders Bjorner and Francesco Brenti, Combinatorics of Coxeter Groups (Graduate Texts in Mathematics 231, Springer 2005; author-hosted complete PDF)",
       "url": "https://sites.math.washington.edu/~billey/classes/reflection.groups/references/EntireBook.pdf",
       "locator": "Section 2.2, printed p. 34 (Corollary 2.2.4: $|[u,w]|\\le 2^{\\ell(w)}$ via the injection into the subwords) and printed p. 35 (Theorem 2.2.6, the Chain Property, and the remark 'Bruhat order is a graded poset whose rank function is the length function; the same is true of every Bruhat interval')"
      },
      {
       "title": "Tom Denton, Lifting property and poset structure of finite Coxeter groups (UC Davis MAT 280 lecture notes, 26 January 2009)",
       "url": "https://www.math.ucdavis.edu/~anne/WQ2009/MAT280-Lecture9.pdf",
       "locator": "Theorem 4 (Chain Property) and the following paragraph (printed pp. 1-2): covers satisfy $\\ell(w)=\\ell(u)+1$ and Bruhat order is a ranked poset with rank function $\\ell$"
      },
      {
       "title": "Carl Marberg, MATH 6150F Coxeter systems and Iwahori-Hecke algebras, Lecture 11: More about Bruhat order (HKUST, Spring 2017)",
       "url": "https://www.math.hkust.edu.hk/~emarberg/teaching/2017/Math6150F/lectures/11_Math6150F_Spring2017.pdf",
       "locator": "Lecture 11, printed pp. 1-2: the chain proposition with its complete two-case induction, the remark bounding all chains by $\\ell(w)-\\ell(v)$, and the corollary that $(W,<)$ is graded with rank function $\\ell$"
      }
     ]
    },
    "dependency_level": 14
