   {
    "id": "thm-cg-bruhat-lifting-and-cover-criterion",
    "kind": "theorem",
    "title": "The lifting property in all four descent cases, the cover criterion, reflection deletion, and directedness",
    "deps": [
     "thm-cg-bruhat-subword-characterization",
     "lem-cg-bruhat-chain-refinement-and-gradedness",
     "lem-cg-bruhat-right-exchange-and-augmentation",
     "def-cg-bruhat-order-by-reflection-chains",
     "thm-hh-coxeter-exchange-deletion-and-faithfulness",
     "def-hh-coxeter-matrix-word-group-and-length",
     "def-group"
    ],
    "statement": "Let $u\\le v$ in $W$, let $s\\in S$, and recall $\\ell(ws)=\\ell(w)\\pm1$ for all $w\\in W$ ([[thm-hh-coxeter-exchange-deletion-and-faithfulness]] (1)).\n\n**(1) Lifting.** All four cases hold:\n(a) if $\\ell(vs)<\\ell(v)$ and $\\ell(us)>\\ell(u)$, then $us\\le v$ and $u\\le vs$;\n(b) if $\\ell(vs)>\\ell(v)$ and $\\ell(us)>\\ell(u)$, then $us\\le vs$, and also $u\\le vs$;\n(c) if $\\ell(vs)<\\ell(v)$ and $\\ell(us)<\\ell(u)$, then $us\\le vs$, and also $us\\le v$;\n(d) if $\\ell(vs)>\\ell(v)$ and $\\ell(us)<\\ell(u)$, then $us\\le v$ and $u\\le vs$.\nThe 'same-direction' cases (b) and (c) are the same-ascent and same-descent variants; (a) is the classical lifting property and (d) is its trivial companion.\n\n**(2) Cover criterion.** For $u\\le v$ the following are equivalent: (i) $u$ is covered by $v$, i.e. $u<v$ and there is no $x$ with $u<x<v$; (ii) $\\ell(v)=\\ell(u)+1$; (iii) $u=vt$ for some reflection $t\\in T$ with $\\ell(vt)=\\ell(v)-1$.\n\n**(3) Reflection deletion.** Let $v=s_1\\cdots s_q$ be a reduced expression and for $i\\in\\{1,\\dots,q\\}$ put $v_i:=s_1\\cdots\\widehat{s_i}\\cdots s_q$ and $t_i:=(s_q\\cdots s_{i+1})s_i(s_{i+1}\\cdots s_q)\\in T$. Then $v_i=vt_i$ and $v_i<v$; moreover $v_i$ is covered by $v$ if and only if $\\ell(v_i)=q-1$, that is, if and only if the word $s_1\\cdots\\widehat{s_i}\\cdots s_q$ is reduced. Conversely every element covered by $v$ is $v_i$ for a uniquely determined $i$, for each fixed reduced expression of $v$; hence the elements covered by $v$ are exactly the distinct values of those single-letter deletions of a reduced expression of $v$ whose remaining word is reduced.\n\n**(4) Directedness.** Bruhat order on $W$ is directed: for all $u,w\\in W$ there is $z\\in W$ with $u\\le z$ and $w\\le z$.",
    "strategy": "Fix a reduced expression $v=s_1\\cdots s_q$ and use the subword criterion ([[thm-cg-bruhat-subword-characterization]]) without further mention.\n\n**(1a)** Since $\\ell(vs)<\\ell(v)$, the word $s_1\\cdots s_q s$ is a reduced expression of $vs$. By the subword criterion $u=s_{i_1}\\cdots s_{i_k}$ is a reduced subword of $s_1\\cdots s_q s$. Its last letter is not $s$: if $s_{i_k}=s$ then $u=u's$ with $\\ell(u')=\\ell(u)-1$, so $\\ell(us)=\\ell(u')=\\ell(u)-1$, contradicting $\\ell(us)>\\ell(u)$. Hence $i_k\\le q$, so $u$ is a reduced subword of $s_1\\cdots s_q$, giving $u\\le vs$; and $us=s_{i_1}\\cdots s_{i_k}s$ is the product of the subword at positions $i_1<\\cdots<i_k<q+1$ of the reduced word $s_1\\cdots s_q s$ of $v$, giving $us\\le v$. This is Bjorner-Brenti, Proposition 2.2.7, in the present right-multiplication convention.\n\n**(1b)** Here $s_1\\cdots s_q s$ is a reduced expression of $vs$ (because $\\ell(vs)=\\ell(v)+1$). The reduced subword expression of $u$ inside $s_1\\cdots s_q$ is also a subword of $s_1\\cdots s_q s$, and appending the last position expresses $us$ as the product of a subword of this reduced word of $vs$; hence $us\\le vs$. The extra claim $u\\le vs$ follows from $u\\le v\\le vs$, the last relation because $v\\to vs$ is an edge.\n\n**(1c)** The edge $us\\to u$ gives $us\\le u\\le v$, and $s$ is an ascent of $us$ because $\\ell(uss)=\\ell(u)>\\ell(us)$. Applying case (1a) to the pair $us\\le v$ (legitimate: $\\ell(vs)<\\ell(v)$ is the hypothesis and $\\ell((us)s)>\\ell(us)$ was just checked) gives $(us)s\\le v$, which is $u\\le v$, and $us\\le vs$, which is the claim. The extra claim $us\\le v$ is the already established relation $us\\le u\\le v$.\n\n**(1d)** $us\\le u\\le v$ (as $us\\to u$ is an edge) and $u\\le v\\le vs$ (as $v\\to vs$ is an edge).\n\n**(2)** (i)$\\Rightarrow$(ii): if $\\ell(v)>\\ell(u)+1$, the chain refinement lemma (2) produces a saturated chain with an element strictly between $u$ and $v$, so $u$ is not covered. (ii)$\\Rightarrow$(i): if $u<x<v$ then $\\ell(u)<\\ell(x)<\\ell(v)$ by strict length increase, impossible when $\\ell(v)=\\ell(u)+1$. (i)$\\Rightarrow$(iii): the subword criterion expresses $u$ as the product of a reduced subword of $s_1\\cdots s_q$ of length $\\ell(u)=q-1$, i.e. of a single-letter deletion, and part (3) exhibits the reflection $t$ with $u=vt$. (iii)$\\Rightarrow$(i): if $u=vt$ with $t\\in T$ and $\\ell(vt)=\\ell(v)-1$, then $v=ut$ with $t\\in T$ and $\\ell(v)>\\ell(u)$, so $u\\to v$ is an edge and $u<v$ with lengths differing by one; (ii) and hence (i) follow.\n\n**(3)** The cancellation computation of [[lem-cg-bruhat-right-exchange-and-augmentation]] (2) (applied to $v$ in place of $u$ there) gives $v_i=vt_i$ and exhibits $s_1\\cdots\\widehat{s_i}\\cdots s_q$ as a word of length $q-1$ for $v_i$; hence $\\ell(v_i)\\le q-1<q=\\ell(v)$ and $v_i<v$ (with $v=v_it_i$ presenting the edge $v_i\\to v$). By (2), $v_i$ is covered by $v$ exactly when $\\ell(v_i)=q-1$, i.e. exactly when the deletion word is reduced. Conversely, if $x$ is covered by $v$ then $\\ell(x)=q-1$ and the subword criterion exhibits $x$ as the product of a reduced subword of $s_1\\cdots s_q$ of length $q-1$; such a subword omits exactly one position $i$, so $x=v_i$, and $i$ is uniquely determined by $x$.\n\n**(4)** Induct on $\\ell(u)+\\ell(w)$. If $\\ell(u)=0$ then $u=1\\le w$ and $z:=w$ works. Otherwise choose $s\\in S$ with $\\ell(us)<\\ell(u)$ (the last letter of a reduced expression of $u$); by the induction hypothesis applied to $(us,w)$ there is $x$ with $us\\le x$ and $w\\le x$. If $\\ell(xs)<\\ell(x)$, case (1a) applied to $us\\le x$ (with $s$ a descent of $x$ and an ascent of $us$, since $\\ell(uss)=\\ell(u)>\\ell(us)$) gives $u=uss\\le x$, so $z:=x$ works. If $\\ell(xs)>\\ell(x)$, case (1b) applied to $us\\le x$ gives $u=uss\\le xs$, while $w\\le x\\le xs$, so $z:=xs$ works. This is Bjorner-Brenti, Proposition 2.2.9, and Denton, Proposition 7. No use of the Axiom of Choice is made.",
    "provenance": {
     "statement": "literature-derived",
     "proof": "ai-altered"
    },
    "sources": {
     "references": [
      {
       "title": "Anders Bjorner and Francesco Brenti, Combinatorics of Coxeter Groups (Graduate Texts in Mathematics 231, Springer 2005; author-hosted complete PDF)",
       "url": "https://sites.math.washington.edu/~billey/classes/reflection.groups/references/EntireBook.pdf",
       "locator": "Section 2.2, printed pp. 35-36: Proposition 2.2.7 (Lifting Property: $u<w$ and $s\\in D_L(w)\\setminus D_L(u)$ give $u\\le sw$ and $su\\le w$) with its subword proof; the sentence 'by the Chain Property, $u\\lessdot w$ means that $u<w$ and $\\ell(u)+1=\\ell(w)$'; and Proposition 2.2.9 (directedness) with its induction"
      },
      {
       "title": "Carl Marberg, MATH 6150F Coxeter systems and Iwahori-Hecke algebras, Lecture 11: More about Bruhat order (HKUST, Spring 2017)",
       "url": "https://www.math.hkust.edu.hk/~emarberg/teaching/2017/Math6150F/lectures/11_Math6150F_Spring2017.pdf",
       "locator": "Lecture 11, printed p. 1: the lifting lemma 'if $u\\le v$ and $s\\in S$ then $us\\le v$ or $us\\le vs$', the covering lemma, and the gradedness corollary used in (1c)"
      },
      {
       "title": "Tom Denton, Lifting property and poset structure of finite Coxeter groups (UC Davis MAT 280 lecture notes, 26 January 2009)",
       "url": "https://www.math.ucdavis.edu/~anne/WQ2009/MAT280-Lecture9.pdf",
       "locator": "Proposition 5 (Lifting Property, printed pp. 1-2) and Proposition 7 (directedness, printed p. 2) with its two-case induction"
      },
      {
       "title": "Grant T. Barkley, Bruhat order and applications, Lecture 3 (CMND lecture notes, author-hosted)",
       "url": "https://gtbarkley.org/cmnd/Lecture3Notes.pdf",
       "locator": "Lemma 1.4 and Corollary 1.5 (printed pp. 1-2): existence of $u'$ with $u<u'\\le v$ and $\\ell(u')=\\ell(u)+1$, and the consequence $\\ell(v)=\\ell(u)+1$ for covers"
      }
