   {
    "id": "thm-cg-bruhat-parabolic-projection-and-quotients",
    "kind": "theorem",
    "title": "The minimal-coset projection onto W^I is order-preserving, and Bruhat order on the parabolic quotient W^I",
    "deps": [
     "def-cg-parabolic-quotient-and-two-sided-minima",
     "thm-cg-parabolic-intersections-and-coset-factorization",
     "thm-hh-parabolic-minimal-representatives-and-length-additivity",
     "thm-cg-bruhat-subword-characterization",
     "thm-cg-bruhat-lifting-and-cover-criterion",
     "lem-cg-bruhat-chain-refinement-and-gradedness",
     "lem-cg-bruhat-right-exchange-and-augmentation",
     "def-cg-bruhat-order-by-reflection-chains",
     "def-hh-coxeter-matrix-word-group-and-length",
     "def-group"
    ],
    "statement": "Let $I\\subseteq S$ and let $W_I$ and $W^I=\\{w\\in W:\\ell(ws)>\\ell(w)\\ \\text{for all }s\\in I\\}$ be as in [[def-cg-parabolic-quotient-and-two-sided-minima]] (1),(2). Every $w\\in W$ has a unique factorization $w=dv$ with $d\\in W^I$, $v\\in W_I$ and $\\ell(w)=\\ell(d)+\\ell(v)$, and $d$ is the unique element of minimal length in the right coset $wW_I$ ([[thm-hh-parabolic-minimal-representatives-and-length-additivity]] (3), [[thm-cg-parabolic-intersections-and-coset-factorization]] (3)); write $P^I(w):=d$ for this **minimal-coset projection**.\n\n**(1) Order-preservation and minimality.** If $u\\le v$ in $W$ then $P^I(u)\\le P^I(v)$; moreover $P^I(w)\\le w$ for every $w\\in W$, with equality exactly when $w\\in W^I$.\n\n**(2) Covers over the quotient.** If $x\\in W^I$, $y\\in W$ and $x$ is covered by $y$, then either $y\\in W^I$ or $y=xs$ for some $s\\in I$.\n\n**(3) The quotient inherits the subword criterion and its length rank.** Let $u,w\\in W^I$ with $u\\le w$. The subword criterion of [[thm-cg-bruhat-subword-characterization]] applies verbatim, since the order on $W^I$ is by definition the restriction of the order on $W$; in addition there is a chain\n$$u=x_0<x_1<\\cdots<x_k=w,\\qquad x_i\\in W^I,\\qquad \\ell(x_i)=\\ell(u)+i\\quad(0\\le i\\le k),$$so $k=\\ell(w)-\\ell(u)$ and every maximal chain in $[u,w]^I:=[u,w]\\cap W^I$ has exactly $\\ell(w)-\\ell(u)$ steps: the subposet $W^I$ is graded by $\\ell$, and $[u,w]^I$ is finite.\n\n**(4) Directedness and top elements.** $W^I$ is directed: for all $u,w\\in W^I$ there is $z\\in W^I$ with $u\\le z$ and $w\\le z$. If $W^I$ is finite then it has a unique maximum $w_0^I$ and $W^I=[1,w_0^I]^I$. For infinite $W$ the projection is defined and order-preserving exactly as above; no longest element of $W$, and no longest element of a parabolic subgroup $W_I$, is asserted or used, and $W^I$ need not have a maximum.",
    "strategy": "**(1)** This is Bjorner-Brenti, Proposition 2.5.1, in the right-coset convention. First, $P^I(w)\\le w$: the factorization $w=P^I(w)v$ with $\\ell(w)=\\ell(P^I(w))+\\ell(v)$ exhibits $w$ as the end of a chain of edges (or the empty chain when $v=1$) starting at $P^I(w)$. For the order-preservation, prove $P^I(u)\\le P^I(v)$ whenever $u\\le v$ by induction on $\\ell(v)$. Since $P^I(u)\\le u\\le v$, the case $P^I(v)=v$ (that is, $v\\in W^I$) is immediate. Otherwise $v\\notin W^I$, so some $s\\in I$ has $\\ell(vs)<\\ell(v)$ ([[def-cg-parabolic-quotient-and-two-sided-minima]] (2)); as $P^I(u)\\in W^I$ we have $\\ell(P^I(u)s)>\\ell(P^I(u))$, so the lifting property [[thm-cg-bruhat-lifting-and-cover-criterion]] (1a) applied to $P^I(u)\\le v$ gives $P^I(u)\\le vs$. The induction hypothesis applies to the pair $(P^I(u),vs)$, whose second entry has smaller length, and yields $P^I(u)=P^I(P^I(u))\\le P^I(vs)=P^I(v)$, the last equality because $vs$ and $v$ lie in the same right coset $vW_I$ and $P^I$ selects its minimal representative ([[thm-cg-parabolic-intersections-and-coset-factorization]] (3)).\n\n**(2)** This is Bjorner-Brenti, Corollary 2.5.2. If $y\\notin W^I$ then $y\\ne P^I(y)$ and $P^I(y)\\le y$ by (1); hence $x\\le P^I(y)<y$, and since $x$ is covered by $y$ we must have $P^I(y)=x$. The factorization $y=P^I(y)v$ with $v\\in W_I$, $v\\ne1$ and $\\ell(y)=\\ell(P^I(y))+\\ell(v)$ then has $\\ell(v)=\\ell(y)-\\ell(x)=1$, so $v=s\\in I$ and $y=xs$.\n\n**(3)** This is Bjorner-Brenti, Theorem 2.5.5. It suffices to construct $x_1$; the rest follows by induction on the length gap. Choose a reduced expression $w=s_1\\cdots s_q$ and a reduced subword expression $u=s_1\\cdots\\widehat{s_{i_1}}\\cdots\\widehat{s_{i_k}}\\cdots s_q$ (deleted positions $i_1<\\cdots<i_k$) with $i_k$ minimal, and let $x_1$ be the word obtained by restoring position $i_k$. The computation of the augmentation lemma ([[lem-cg-bruhat-right-exchange-and-augmentation]] (2)) shows $u=x_1t$ with $t=(s_q\\cdots s_{i_k+1})s_{i_k}(s_{i_k+1}\\cdots s_q)\\in T$, that $\\ell(x_1)=\\ell(u)+1$ and that $x_1\\le w$; hence $u\\to x_1$ and $x_1$ is the product of a reduced subword of the fixed word of $w$. It remains to check $x_1\\in W^I$. If not, part (2) applied to the cover $u<x_1$ with $u\\in W^I$ gives $x_1=us$ for some $s\\in I$; but $x_1=ut$ forces $t=s$, and then $ws=s_1\\cdots\\widehat{s_{i_k}}\\cdots s_q$ has length $q-1<q$, contradicting $w\\in W^I$ (which requires $\\ell(ws)>\\ell(w)$ for all $s\\in I$). Therefore $x_1\\in W^I$, and the induction applies to $x_1\\le w$ inside $W^I$, producing the required chain; the maximal-chain statement then follows exactly as in [[lem-cg-bruhat-chain-refinement-and-gradedness]] (3), and $[u,w]^I$ is finite because $[u,w]$ is (part (1) there). This is the point at which the quotient does not simply inherit the chain property: the check $x_1\\in W^I$ is an extra argument.\n\n**(4)** Directedness: given $u,w\\in W^I$, the ambient Bruhat order is directed ([[thm-cg-bruhat-lifting-and-cover-criterion]] (4)), so there is $z\\in W$ with $u,w\\le z$; applying the order-preserving projection of (1) and using $P^I(u)=u$, $P^I(w)=w$ gives $u,w\\le P^I(z)\\in W^I$. If $W^I$ is finite, directedness produces a unique maximum: a common upper bound of all elements is maximal, and two maximal elements bound each other, so they coincide; then $W^I=[1,w_0^I]^I$ by (1). No longest element of $W_I$ is used anywhere: the factorization of [[thm-hh-parabolic-minimal-representatives-and-length-additivity]] (3) is unconditional, and in the infinite case the supremum of $W^I$ need not exist. No use of the Axiom of Choice is made.",
    "provenance": {
     "statement": "literature-derived",
     "proof": "ai-altered"
    },
    "sources": {
     "references": [
      {
       "title": "Anders Bjorner and Francesco Brenti, Combinatorics of Coxeter Groups (Graduate Texts in Mathematics 231, Springer 2005; author-hosted complete PDF)",
       "url": "https://sites.math.washington.edu/~billey/classes/reflection.groups/references/EntireBook.pdf",
       "locator": "Section 2.5, printed pp. 42-44: Proposition 2.5.1 (the projection $P^J$ is order-preserving) with its induction proof, Corollary 2.5.2 (covers over $W^J$), Corollary 2.5.3 ($W^J$ is directed), Theorem 2.5.5 (the Chain Property in $W^J$, including the extra check that the constructed $w_1$ lies in $W^J$) and Corollary 2.5.6 (all maximal chains from $u$ to $w$ in $W^J$ have the same length)"
      },
      {
       "title": "Carl Marberg, MATH 6150F Coxeter systems and Iwahori-Hecke algebras, Lecture 11: More about Bruhat order (HKUST, Spring 2017)",
       "url": "https://www.math.hkust.edu.hk/~emarberg/teaching/2017/Math6150F/lectures/11_Math6150F_Spring2017.pdf",
       "locator": "Lecture 11, printed p. 2: the proposition that each $w$ has a unique $u\\in W^J$, $v\\in W_J$ with $w=uv$ and $\\ell(w)=\\ell(u)+\\ell(v)$, that $u$ is the unique shortest element of $wW_J$, and the corollary $\\ell(uv)=\\ell(u)+\\ell(v)$ for $u\\in W^J$, $v\\in W_J$"
      }
     ]
    },
    "dependency_level": 16
   }
  ]
 },
