    "statement": "Let $W=S_4$ with simple reflections $s_1=(1\\,2)$, $s_2=(2\\,3)$, $s_3=(3\\,4)$, so that $\\ell$ is the inversion number ([[thm-hh-parabolic-minimal-representatives-and-length-additivity]] (4), [[def-finite-symmetric-group-and-permutation-notation]], [[def-inversions-inversion-number-and-sign]]); write permutations in one-line notation. Let $w_0=4321$ be the longest element and use its reduced expression $w_0=s_1s_2s_3s_1s_2s_1$.\n\n**(i)** The element $u:=s_1s_3=2143$ satisfies $u\\le w_0$: the subword at positions $1,3$ of $s_1s_2s_3s_1s_2s_1$ is $s_1s_3$, a reduced expression of $u$ ([[thm-cg-bruhat-subword-characterization]]).\n\n**(ii)** The $2^6=64$ subwords of $s_1s_2s_3s_1s_2s_1$ realize exactly $24=4!$ distinct elements, namely all of $S_4=[1,w_0]$. For example the position sets $\\{1\\}$, $\\{3\\}$, $\\{1,3\\}$, $\\{2,3\\}$, $\\{1,2,3\\}$, $\\{1,2,3,5\\}$ and $\\{1,2,3,4,5,6\\}$ realize $s_1=2134$, $s_3=1243$, $s_1s_3=2143$, $s_2s_3=1342$, $s_1s_2s_3=2341$, $s_1s_2s_3s_2=2431$ and $w_0=4321$; and the element $s_1=2134$ alone arises from the position sets $\\{1\\}$, $\\{4\\}$, $\\{6\\}$, $\\{1,2,5\\}$, $\\{1,4,6\\}$ and $\\{2,5,6\\}$, so different subwords of one reduced expression may realize the same element.\n\n**(iii)** Reflection deletions. Deleting the $i$-th letter of $s_1s_2s_3s_1s_2s_1$ realizes the following elements: $4312$, $4231$ and $3421$ of length $5$ for $i=1,4,6$; $4123$ and $2341$ of length $3$ for $i=2,5$; and $1324$ of length $1$ for $i=3$. By [[thm-cg-bruhat-lifting-and-cover-criterion]] (3) each of these equals $w_0t_i$ for the reflection $t_i$ conjugated to $s_i$, and each lies strictly below $w_0$; exactly the first three are covers of $w_0$ (their remaining words are reduced of length $5=\\ell(w_0)-1$), while the other three deletion words are not reduced and realize much shorter elements. Consistently the covers of $w_0$ are precisely the three elements of length $5$ in $S_4$ ([[thm-cg-bruhat-lifting-and-cover-criterion]] (2)), and no element of length $<5$ can be covered by $w_0$.",
    "strategy": "All assertions are finite computations in $S_4$, using $\\ell=$ inversion number ([[thm-hh-parabolic-minimal-representatives-and-length-additivity]] (4)) and the subword and cover criteria ([[thm-cg-bruhat-subword-characterization]], [[thm-cg-bruhat-lifting-and-cover-criterion]]).\n\n**(i)** Multiplying the letters at positions $1$ and $3$ of $s_1s_2s_3s_1s_2s_1$ gives $s_1s_3$; since $s_1$ and $s_3$ act on disjoint pairs of positions they commute and $\\ell(s_1s_3)=2=\\ell(s_1)+\\ell(s_3)$, so $s_1s_3$ is reduced and the subword criterion gives $u\\le w_0$.\n\n**(ii)** The interval notation $[1,w_0]$ is that of [[lem-cg-bruhat-chain-refinement-and-gradedness]], and $S_4=[1,w_0]$: Bruhat order is directed ([[thm-cg-bruhat-lifting-and-cover-criterion]] (4)), a finite directed poset has a greatest element (a common upper bound of a maximal element and any further element forces equality), and by strict length increase such a greatest element must be the unique longest element $w_0$ ([[def-cg-bruhat-order-by-reflection-chains]] (2)); finally $1\\le w_0$ by the identity clause of that item. Hence every subword value lies in $S_4$, and enumerating the $64$ subwords shows that all $24$ elements occur. Each displayed evaluation is the direct product of the indicated letters, and the six listed position sets for $s_1$ are read off the word: positions $1,4,6$ carry $s_1$, and the three $4$-letter sets are obtained by adjoining one further commuting copy of $s_1$; verifying that the length of each displayed value equals its word length identifies the reduced expressions.\n\n**(iii)** Each deletion is multiplied out; the identity $v_i=vt_i$ with $t_i$ the reflection conjugated to $s_i$ is the cancellation of the tail $s_{i+1}\\cdots s_6$ with its inverse, as in [[thm-cg-bruhat-lifting-and-cover-criterion]] (3). The values $4312$, $4231$, $3421$ have length $5$ and are covers by the cover criterion (2); the values $4123$, $2341$, $1324$ have lengths $3,3,1<5$, so their deletion words are not reduced and they cannot be covers of $w_0$, whose covers all have length $5$. This is finite evidence of consistency of the general theorems, within its computed scope; the general statements are proved on the A page. No use of the Axiom of Choice is made.",
