    ],
    "statement": "In $W=S_4$ with simple reflections $s_i=(i\\ i+1)$, one-line notation and $\\ell$ the inversion number ([[thm-hh-parabolic-minimal-representatives-and-length-additivity]] (4)), the four cases of the lifting property ([[thm-cg-bruhat-lifting-and-cover-criterion]] (1)) occur as follows (products are right multiplication, $ws_i$ swapping the entries in positions $i$ and $i+1$).\n\n**(a)** $u=1342$ ($\\ell=2$), $v=4132$ ($\\ell=4$), $s=s_1$: $us=3142$ ($\\ell=3$) and $vs=1432$ ($\\ell=3$); here $s$ is a descent of $v$ and an ascent of $u$, and indeed $us=3142\\le v=4132$ and $u=1342\\le vs=1432$, while $us\\le vs$ fails: the two lifted elements are incomparable.\n\n**(b)** $u=1342$, $v=4132$, $s=s_2$: $us=1432$ ($\\ell=3$) and $vs=4312$ ($\\ell=5$); here $s$ is an ascent of both, and $us=1432\\le vs=4312$.\n\n**(c)** $u=1342$ ($\\ell=2$), $v=1432$ ($\\ell=3$), $s=s_3$: $us=1324$ ($\\ell=1$) and $vs=1423$ ($\\ell=2$); here $s$ is a descent of both, and $us=1324\\le vs=1423$, while $u\\le vs$ fails.\n\n**(d)** $u=1423$ ($\\ell=2$), $v=4123$ ($\\ell=3$), $s=s_2$: $us=1243$ ($\\ell=1$) and $vs=4213$ ($\\ell=4$); here $s$ is a descent of $u$ and an ascent of $v$, and $us=1243\\le v=4123$, $u=1423\\le vs=4213$.\n\nIn each case $u,v,us,vs$ are the four vertices of a Bruhat square whose sides are $u\\le v$, $us\\le vs$ or $us\\le v$ or $u\\le vs$ according to the case; cases (a) and (c) show that the case hypotheses are exactly what each conclusion needs: in (a) the comparison $us\\le vs$ is false and in (c) the comparison $u\\le vs$ is false.",
    "strategy": "All assertions are finite computations in $S_4$ with $\\ell$ the inversion number ([[def-inversions-inversion-number-and-sign]], [[thm-hh-parabolic-minimal-representatives-and-length-additivity]] (4)); comparability is decided by the subword criterion ([[thm-cg-bruhat-subword-characterization]]). Multiplying a permutation on the right by $s_i$ swaps its entries in positions $i$ and $i+1$, so the displayed values of $us$ and $vs$ are direct computations and their lengths are inversion numbers.\n\nThe positive comparisons are certified by subword witnesses in the displayed reduced words: $3142=s_2s_1s_3$ is the subword at positions $1,2,4$ of $4132=s_2s_3s_2s_1$; $1342=s_2s_3$ is the subword at positions $1,2$ of $1432=s_2s_3s_2$; $1432=s_2s_3s_2$ is the subword at positions $1,3,4$ of $4312=s_2s_1s_3s_2s_1$; $1324=s_2$ is the subword at position $2$ of $1423=s_3s_2$; $1243=s_3$ is the subword at position $1$ of $4123=s_3s_2s_1$; and $1423=s_3s_2$ is the subword at positions $2,3$ of $4213=s_1s_3s_2s_1$.\n\nThe two negative comparisons follow from length alone: $us=3142$ and $vs=1432$ both have length $3$, and $1342$ and $vs=1423$ both have length $2$; distinct elements of equal length are never comparable (clause (2) of [[def-cg-bruhat-order-by-reflection-chains]]), and $3142\\ne1432$, $1342\\ne1423$.\n\nThe descent and ascent patterns are read off the listed lengths: in (a) $\\ell(vs)=3<4=\\ell(v)$ and $\\ell(us)=3>2=\\ell(u)$; in (b) $\\ell(vs)=5>4$ and $\\ell(us)=3>2$; in (c) $\\ell(vs)=2<3$ and $\\ell(us)=1<2$; and in (d) $\\ell(vs)=4>3$ and $\\ell(us)=1<2$. No use of the Axiom of Choice is made: all verifications are finite.",
